The function is increasing, so any intersection lies on $y=x$.
$\begin{aligned}x^2-6&=x\\x^2-x-6&=0\\(x-3)(x+2)&=0\\x&=3\text{ or }-2\end{aligned}$
Reject $x=-2$ because the original domain requires $x\ge0$. The intersection is $(3,3)$.
The inverse is $f^{-1}(x)=\sqrt{x+6}$, $x\ge-6$. The endpoints $(0,-6)$ and $(-6,0)$ are reflections in $y=x$.
