The function is increasing, so any intersection lies on $y=x$. Since the square root is non-negative, an intersection requires $x\ge0$.
$\begin{aligned}\sqrt{x+6}&=x\\x+6&=x^2\\x^2-x-6&=0\\(x-3)(x+2)&=0\\x&=3\text{ or }-2\end{aligned}$
Reject $x=-2$: $\sqrt{-2+6}=2$, which is not $-2$. For $x=3$, $\sqrt{3+6}=3$. The intersection is $(3,3)$.
The inverse is $f^{-1}(x)=x^2-6$, $x\ge0$.
